# Riomhdhos OSC feedback pattern - rig -> desktop, for driving visuals.
#
# WHY NOT Default.ReaperOSC: it enables FX parameter feedback, and this rig has a
# free-running drum sequencer whose "armed mask" slider changes continuously. REAPER
# faithfully reports every change as the last-touched FX param, which measured at
# ~300 messages/second, forever, with the transport stopped. That is the entire OSC
# stream: track and transport feedback never got through.
#
# A control link that is 100% noise is worse than no link, because it looks alive.
#
# Anything not listed here is simply not sent. That is the point: this file is a
# whitelist, and the traffic is whatever the visuals actually need.

# Track count covers the rig's 11 tracks with headroom. Feedback is only sent for
# tracks inside the bank, so a count smaller than the project silently hides the
# tracks past the end - which looks like "OSC is broken" rather than "bank too small".
DEVICE_TRACK_COUNT 16

# ⚠️ THE FIX. All four of these must be 0. FX_COUNT alone is not enough - the
# last-touched-parameter feedback is governed by the param counts, and that is where
# the flood came from.
DEVICE_FX_COUNT 0
DEVICE_FX_PARAM_COUNT 0
DEVICE_FX_INST_PARAM_COUNT 0
DEVICE_SEND_COUNT 0
DEVICE_RECEIVE_COUNT 0
DEVICE_MARKER_COUNT 0
DEVICE_REGION_COUNT 0

DEVICE_TRACK_FOLLOWS DEVICE
DEVICE_TRACK_BANK_FOLLOWS DEVICE

# ---- transport ----------------------------------------------------------------
PLAY t/play
STOP t/stop
TEMPO n/tempo s/tempo/str

# ---- per-track state ------------------------------------------------------------
# The mood is derived from the MUTES, not from a dedicated message: Riomhdhos_mood_mute
# owns REAPER's track mutes and re-asserts the active mood every 30 ms, so exactly one
# mood track is unmuted at any moment. That makes /track/@/mute the authoritative
# statement of which mood is live, and it needs no new plumbing on the rig.
#
# ⚠️ It also means anything that fights mood_mute loses. Setting a mood track's mute
# from outside is reverted within a frame - and the OSC feedback for the change may
# never flush, so it looks like OSC is broken when the write is what failed.
TRACK_NAME s/track/@/name
TRACK_MUTE b/track/@/mute
TRACK_SELECT b/track/@/select
TRACK_VOLUME n/track/@/volume f/track/@/volume/db
